# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The app is hosted now. There is no browser-only mode to fall back to, so
# without these four it does not run: it signs nobody in and has nowhere to keep
# a workspace.

# Any Postgres works: Neon, Supabase, Railway. Use the POOLED connection string
# on serverless, or connections are exhausted within minutes.
#
#   npm run db:studio    browse the data
#
# Migrations are plain SQL in drizzle/. drizzle-kit generate wants a TTY for its
# rename prompts, so they are written by hand and applied directly.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host/dbname?sslmode=require

# Auth switches itself on only when all three AUTH_ values are present.
#
# 1. Generate the secret:      npx auth secret
# 2. Create an OAuth client:   https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
#    Application type:         Web application
#    Authorised redirect URI:  http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google
#                              https://YOUR-DOMAIN/api/auth/callback/google
AUTH_SECRET=
AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=

# Whoever runs this deployment. Separated by commas, spaces, semicolons, or
# newlines, because an environment field in a dashboard is a textarea and one
# address a line is the obvious guess.
#
# This is not the same thing as running a business on the panel. Every
# customer's first member is an administrator of their own workspace; an
# operator can read and delete every workspace there is. The list is kept in the
# environment rather than the database so it cannot be granted by anything the
# app itself does.
#
# Leave it empty on a fresh install and the first person to sign in becomes the
# operator. That window closes the moment a row exists.
OPERATOR_EMAILS=

# The master key the businesses' own model keys are encrypted with, so what the
# settings table holds is ciphertext and a leaked connection string, a stolen
# backup, or a dump handed to the wrong person is a file of useless strings
# rather than working credentials.
#
# 32 bytes, base64. Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#
# Set it before anybody stores a key. Set it afterwards and run
# npm run keys-encrypt, which sweeps up whatever is still in plaintext and
# verifies every row round trips before it writes.
#
# Losing it means every business re-enters its key. It does not lock anyone
# out of the panel and nothing else in the database depends on it, but there is
# no recovering the keys themselves: that is the point of it not being in the
# database. Keep a copy somewhere that is not this deployment.
KEY_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deployment
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The canonical origin. Set this in production so the link in an invitation
# never points at a preview deployment. eterneon.net is the portfolio; this app
# lives on its own subdomain.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://business.eterneon.net

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Invitations
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Without these, adding somebody still works. The access row is what grants
# entry; the email only tells them it is there. They just have to be told some
# other way.
#
# https://resend.com
RESEND_API_KEY=

# Who invitations come from. Has to be on a domain verified in Resend, or the
# send is refused. Left unset it falls back to Resend's shared sender, which
# only delivers to the address that owns the API key, so a misconfigured
# deployment fails visibly in testing rather than silently in front of a
# customer.
#
#   npm run email-preview    writes the invitation to a file to look at
INVITE_FROM=invites@yourdomain.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The conduct reviewer
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Operator, Reports. Reads internal messages for harassment, threats, fraud,
# malware, and anyone who may be at risk. Unset, the feature is simply off and
# the screen says so.
#
# This is deliberately its own variable and not ANTHROPIC_API_KEY. That one is
# checked before a workspace's own key, so setting it to give the reviewer
# something to run on would quietly take every customer off their own key and
# put the whole deployment's chat spend on yours.
#
# Tell your people this exists before relying on it. Several jurisdictions
# require notice for workplace monitoring, and it is the difference between a
# safety net and something that feels like surveillance when it is discovered.
REVIEWER_API_KEY=sk-ant-...

# The reviewer runs on its own, once a day, through the cron entry in
# vercel.json. Vercel sends this as a bearer token on a scheduled call, and the
# route refuses everything else, so a job that spends money on every invocation
# is not left open to anyone who guesses the path.
#
# Vercel can generate this for you when you add it. Without it the scheduled
# call is refused and only the button in Operator, Reports still works.
#
# The schedule is daily at 03:00 UTC because that is what every Vercel plan
# allows. On Pro you can make it hourly by changing vercel.json to "0 * * * *".
CRON_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Model keys (optional, and usually left unset on a multi-tenant deployment)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The order is: this environment first, then the business's own key, then a key
# typed into a browser. So setting one of these takes every customer off their
# own key and bills the whole deployment to you. That is right for a
# single-tenant install and wrong for a product.
#
# A business sets its own under Settings, and only an administrator can. They
# are write-only: nothing returns a key, to them or to you, only whether one
# exists and its last four characters.
#
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
# GEMINI_API_KEY=...

# Only for an identity-linked Anthropic key, which rejects any request that does
# not name the workspace it acts in. Anthropic Console, Settings, Workspaces.
# Applies to the environment key above, never to a key a business set itself.
# ANTHROPIC_WORKSPACE_ID=wrkspc_...
